    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d216">

                <pb facs="0334" n="254" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg254"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Mutual Guarantee (Locarno)/471:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Biddle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Oslo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-18T17:00:00+01:00">March 18, 1936—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 18—12:47 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>20. In the belief that the Department may be interested I venture to report
                    consolidation of information received from sources I consider authoritative from
                    which it becomes evident that the British Government will propose at intervals
                    during the next 12 months various portions of a most comprehensive peace plan of
                    realistic character constituting veritable European new deal providing <hi
                        rendition="#italic">inter alia</hi> for equality for Germany and security
                    for France and Belgium.</p>
                <p>Summary of plan for which Neville Chamberlain<anchor n="8" xml:id="d216-f1"/> is
                    largely responsible follows: Tension now existing as a result of Italo-Ethiopian
                    conflict and Franco-German differences will first be relieved through cessation
                    of African hostilities on realistic basis. Sanctions to be suppressed against
                    Italy and excluded from application to Germany while the Franco-Russian pact is
                    eventually to be referred to Hague Tribunal. Secondly, a series of pacification
                    pacts between Western Europe as suggested by German Chancellor and also a new
                    Locarno providing for international patrol forces in Rhineland. Finally,
                    convocation of a general conference on armament for the purpose of obtaining
                    promise of annual report of changes contemplated by each country in order that
                    element of surprise may be removed from international military situation.
                    Participation of the United States in such a conference believed to be
                    possible.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Biddle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
